Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-10-2001||11:57 AM||||When you switch your engine over to a carbrated engine..and use the fuel pump on the engine.. does the intank have to be removed or just unplugged?<P>A few years back when I was using my fiero as a daily driver and my 4 banger decided to die, I had to install a 2.8L carbed engine on my day off,I love having the carb on the car ( had too much problems with the TBI) <BR>but when I leave her sitting any longer then a day, she starts losing her gas out of the lines.. Takes forever to start it then..   Is it sucking air through the intank one?<BR>I dont mind removing it, but would like to know for sure before I spend the time removing it.<P> All lines are tight... Actually a new fuel pump on here now, and no leaks anywhere.<P>Thanks!<P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 06-10-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-10-2001||10:19 PM||||anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||grizco||06-11-2001||12:17 AM||||Are you pumping the gas before you try to start the car? Sounds like when you leave the car sit for more than a day the gas is evaporating out the vent in the float bowl, so when you try to start it the engine mounted fuel pump has to pump the gas from the tank to the carb before the engine will start. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-11-2001||08:25 AM||||Everythings completely dry, from the carb to the gas lines..<P>Its like starting a  car that ran out of gas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>It must be getting air somewhere and not holding its gas in the fuel pump.?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Chris McDonald||06-11-2001||01:08 PM||||The stock fuel pump uses a signal from the computer during the first ignition on sequence to pulse the fuel pump for a few seconds.  That primes the injector.  Then, there is a a signal that comes through the oil pressure switch indicating the engine is actually running that goes back to the computer and keeps the fuel pump running.  <P>You obviously have the pump wired or it would not run at all.  With the carb, the other comments here are correct.  You will have some minor amount of evaporation.  However, it tuned proper, a turn of the key you should hear the pump hum and shut off.  Pump the pedal and start.  It should crank.<P>Make sure all the computer signals are working proper and make sure you are getting the initial pumping action when you first turn the key on.  You should also hear the pump hum for a few seconds if you just bump the starter.  <P>On my V8 car, I put a pressure regulator on the feed line and cut it down around 3.5 pounds.  I simply plugged the return line.  It works just fine that way.  We even supercharged a S-15 with a carb and did the same thing with 5.5 psi of pressure on the regulator.<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-11-2001||03:22 PM||||I unplugged the intank one completly, And using the external manual pump on the block. IF I leave the car for a day, and took my gasline off, it would be completly dry right to the tank.. <BR>would having the old intank one sitting idle inside the tank be causing an airleak?<BR>I tried another carb and it does the same thing.. also it has a new fuel pump..  It never bothered me before because I drove it daily.. Now since its only for when I have time, Its annoying to have to either a: prime it b: pump and rotate the motor for a few minutes until it sucks the fuel up.<BR>Should I just replace the lines completely to the tank(including inside) and remove the electric pump.. or bypass the manual pump and use the electric?<P>Any way to hook the electric up to give it that extra shot when its cold? (to force gas into the pump and lines?)<P>Thanks again!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||grizco||06-12-2001||12:46 AM||||Removing the electric fuel pump from the tank will have no effect on the fuel evaporating from the carborator and the gas lines going to the tank when the car sits for more than a day. My 1970 Chevy Chevelle has a carborator and mechanical fuel pump and if I drive my motorcycle for a few days and don't start the Chevelle I have to pump the gas 15-20 time before it will start. Even if it only sits overnight the first time I start it for the day I have to pump the gas 3 times for it to start. If you want it to start without pumping the gas you'll have to find a way to wire the electric pump so it turns on and off with the ignition without it running unil it builds up 14 psi of pressure when you turn the car off. If it runs on after you turn the car off it may over fill the float bowl and flood the carb and the excess gas could then make its way into your oil.<P>If you decide to scrap the mechanical pump you'll have to put a fuel pressure regulator set to less than 7 psi inline before the carb or the car will run rich.<P>josh<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-12-2001||07:24 AM||||Thanks for the info.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chris McDonald||06-12-2001||12:32 PM||||I did not realize you are running the mechanical pump.  If tuned proper, you should not have such a long start up mode.  It is possible that the mechanical pump has a problem in the diaphram.  Try putting a glass or transparent fuel filter in line between the carb and pump.  It should retain fuel in it for a very long time after you shut down.  The mechanical fuel pump should only feed in one direction.  Yours may be leaking back into the tank.<P>Second suggestion.  Check the float level on your new and old carb.  Make sure it is high enough to keep an ample amount of fuel on hand.  <P>Finally, or what should be first.  Start the engine and then let it sit for a few days.  Pull the air cleaner and manually pull the throttle while looking down the throat of the carb.  You should see fuel squirt.  If none is squirting, go back and replace the pump--and or set the float level higher.  <P>I agree with the other response, and that is one reason why fuel injection is so much better than a carb.  However, your carb with the proper choke setting (YOU DO HAVE A CHOKE---RIGHT?), float level, no vacuum leaks, and a good tight mechanical fuel pump should fire right up with one or two swift pumps on the pedal even after the car has  been sitting.  <P>Good luck.||||reg||1
